"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,529\nThe Learning Brain. Series 5: Belonging\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,351\nEpisode 3\n\n3\n00:00:07,431 --> 00:00:09,390\nWhat the room says\n\n4\n00:00:12,190 --> 00:00:13,477\nWalk into a room and,\n\n5\n00:00:13,497 --> 00:00:17,112\nwithin seconds, you sense whether people like you belong there.\n\n6\n00:00:17,529 --> 00:00:19,951\nIt happens before anyone says a word.\n\n7\n00:00:20,462 --> 00:00:22,868\nResearchers call it ambient belonging.\n\n8\n00:00:23,038 --> 00:00:24,747\nIn a 2009 study,\n\n9\n00:00:24,867 --> 00:00:29,888\nstudents who were not studying computer science filled out a career survey in a small classroom.\n\n10\n00:00:30,463 --> 00:00:34,830\nFor some, the room held objects stereotyped as computer science: a Star Trek poster,\n\n11\n00:00:35,201 --> 00:00:38,208\nvideo game boxes, soda cans.\n\n12\n00:00:38,317 --> 00:00:42,815\nFor others, the same room held a nature poster,\n\n13\n00:00:43,044 --> 00:00:45,502\nwater bottles, and general magazines.\n\n14\n00:00:45,672 --> 00:00:50,400\nWomen in the stereotyped room reported less interest in computer science.\n\n15\n00:00:50,772 --> 00:00:51,868\nIn the other room,\n\n16\n00:00:52,068 --> 00:00:54,546\ntheir interest rose to the level of men's.\n\n17\n00:00:54,909 --> 00:00:57,350\nMen's interest did not change.\n\n18\n00:00:57,520 --> 00:01:04,666\nIn another study, women chose between two all female teams that differed only in the objects in their workrooms.\n\n19\n00:01:05,109 --> 00:01:09,137\nEighty two percent picked the team without the stereotyped objects.\n\n20\n00:01:09,397 --> 00:01:11,896\nSo it was not only about who was in the room.\n\n21\n00:01:12,275 --> 00:01:13,847\nIt was what the room said.\n\n22\n00:01:14,017 --> 00:01:18,309\nThe objects broadcast a narrow image of who does computer science.\n\n23\n00:01:18,704 --> 00:01:20,954\nWomen felt less sense of belonging there,\n\n24\n00:01:21,206 --> 00:01:23,544\nand that is what lowered their interest.\n\n25\n00:01:23,714 --> 00:01:24,692\nSome caution.\n\n26\n00:01:25,067 --> 00:01:27,616\nThese were short studies with college students,\n\n27\n00:01:27,654 --> 00:01:30,168\nabout one field and one stereotype.\n\n28\n00:01:30,677 --> 00:01:32,953\nObjects are one cue among many.\n\n29\n00:01:33,201 --> 00:01:35,254\nBut cues are cheap to change.\n\n30\n00:01:35,424 --> 00:01:36,175\nFor teaching.\n\n31\n00:01:36,499 --> 00:01:37,528\nLook at your room,\n\n32\n00:01:37,548 --> 00:01:40,321\nyour slides, and your examples with fresh eyes.\n\n33\n00:01:40,603 --> 00:01:43,175\nWho appears in your images and case studies?\n\n34\n00:01:43,491 --> 00:01:46,262\nVary the names and contexts in your problems.\n\n35\n00:01:46,562 --> 00:01:48,835\nShow the range of people who do your field.\n\n36\n00:01:49,240 --> 00:01:51,950\nAnd ask a few students what your space says to them.\n\n37\n00:01:52,374 --> 00:01:57,454\nOne tip to share with students: a room's look is not a verdict on whether you fit.\n\n38\n00:01:57,624 --> 00:01:59,240\nEvery room sends a message.\n\n39\n00:01:59,388 --> 00:02:00,531\nChoose yours.\n\n"
